"GitHub - w3c/browser-specs: A machine-readable list of Web specifications · GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/browser-specs)\nciteturn1378view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/w3c/browser-specs\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 747\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - w3c/webref: Machine-readable references of terms defined in web browser specifications · GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/webref)\nciteturn1378view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/w3c/webref\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 413\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Color Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-4/)\nciteturn1378view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view0\",\"pattern\":\"missing components\"}); Total lines: 5518\nL91:     12. cite76†10.12 Converting Between Predefined RGB Color Spaces L92:     13. cite77†10.13 Simple Alpha Compositing L93:   11. cite78†11 Converting Colors L94:     1. cite79†11.1 Introduction to Converting Colors L95:     2. cite80†11.2 Algorithm for Converting Colors L96:   12. cite81†12 Comparing <color> Values L97:   13. cite82†13 Color Interpolation L98:     1. cite83†13.1 Component-wise Linear Interpolation L99:     2. cite84†13.2 Color Space for Interpolation L100:     3. cite85†13.3 Interpolating with Missing Components L101:     4. cite86†13.4 Interpolating with Alpha L102:     5. cite87†13.5 Hue Interpolation L103:       1. cite88†13.5.1 shorter L104:       2. cite89†13.5.2 longer L105:       3. cite90†13.5.3 increasing L106:       4. cite91†13.5.4 decreasing L107:   14. cite92†14 Gamut Mapping L505: #### 4.1.1. Modern (Space-separated) Color Function Syntax\nL506: \nL507: All of the cite375†absolute color functional forms first defined in this specification use the modern color syntax, meaning:\nL508: \nL509:   * color components are separated by whitespace\nL510: \nL511:   * the optional alpha term is separated by a solidus (\"/\")\nL512: \nL513:   * minimum required precision cite114†when serializing is defined, and may be greater than 8 bits per component\nL514: \nL515:   * the cite376†none value is allowed, to represent cite377†missing components L516:   * components using cite298†<percentage> and cite296†<number> may be freely mixed\nL517: \nL518: The following represents a saturated sRGB red that is 50% opaque:\nL519: \nL520:     rgb(100% 0% 0% / 50%)\nL593:   * cite415†color-invalid-rgb.html†wpt.fyi cite416†(live test)†wpt.live cite417†(source)†github.com L594:   * cite418†color-valid-color-function.html†wpt.fyi cite419†(live test)†wpt.live cite420†(source)†github.com L595:   * cite421†color-valid-color-mix-function.html†wpt.fyi cite422†(live test)†wpt.live cite423†(source)†github.com L596:   * cite424†color-valid-hsl.html†wpt.fyi cite425†(live test)†wpt.live cite426†(source)†github.com L597:   * cite427†color-valid-hwb.html†wpt.fyi cite428†(live test)†wpt.live cite429†(source)†github.com L598:   * cite430†color-valid-lab.html†wpt.fyi cite431†(live test)†wpt.live cite432†(source)†github.com L599:   * cite433†color-valid-relative-color.html†wpt.fyi cite434†(live test)†wpt.live cite435†(source)†github.com L600:   * cite436†color-valid-rgb.html†wpt.fyi cite437†(live test)†wpt.live cite438†(source)†github.com L601: For handling of cite377†missing components in situations which combine two colors, such as color interpolation, see cite85†§ 13.3 Interpolating with Missing Components .\nL602: \nL603: For all other purposes, a cite377†missing component behaves as a zero value, in the appropriate unit for that component: 0, 0%, or 0deg. This includes rendering the color directly, converting it to another color space, performing computations on the color component values, etc.\nL604: If a color with a cite377†missing component is serialized or otherwise presented directly to an author, then for cite378†legacy color syntax it represents that component as a zero value; otherwise, it represents that component as being the cite376†none keyword.\nL605: A missing hue is common when interpolating in cylindrical color spaces. For example, using the cite439†color-mix() function specified in cite440†[CSS-COLOR-5] one could write color-mix(in hsl, white 30%, green 70%).\nL606: Since cite384†white is an achromatic color, it has a cite377†missing hue when expressed in cite355†hsl() (effectively hsl(none 0% 100%)), since any hue will produce the same color) which means that the color-mix function will treat it as having the same hue as cite441†green (effectively hsl(120deg 0% 100%)), and then interpolate based on those components.\nL607: The result will be a color that truly looks like a blend of green and white, rather than perhaps looking reddish (if cite384†white s hue was defaulted to 0deg).\nL608: \nL609: Explicitly specifying missing components can be useful to achieve an effect where you only want to interpolate certain components of a color.\nL610: For example, to animate a color to \"grayscale\", no matter what the color is, one can interpolate it with oklch(none 0 none). This will take the hue and lightness from the starting color, but animate its chroma down to 0, rendering it into an equal-lightness gray with a steady hue across the whole animation.\nL611: \nL612: Doing this manually would require matching the hue and lightness of the starting color explicitly.\nL613: #### 4.4.1. “Powerless” Color Components\nL614: \nL615: Individual color syntaxes can specify that, in some cases, a given component of their syntax becomes a powerless color component. This indicates that the value of the component doesn’t affect the rendered color; any value you give it will result in the same color displayed in the screen.\nL616: For example, in cite355†hsl() , the hue component is cite383†powerless when the saturation component is 0%; a 0% saturation indicates a grayscale color, which has no hue at all, so 0deg and 180deg, or any other angle, will give the exact same result.\nL617: \nL618: If a cite383†powerless component is manually specified, it acts as normal; the fact that it’s powerless has no effect.\nL619: However, if a color is automatically produced by color space conversion, then any cite383†powerless components in the result must instead be set to cite377†missing , instead of whatever value was produced by the conversion process.\nL2419:   1. checking the two colors for cite443†analogous components and cite1134†analogous sets which will be carried forward\nL2420:   2. (if conversion is required) changing any cite383†powerless components to cite377†missing values\nL2421:   3. (if required) converting them both to a given color space which will be referred to as the interpolation color space below.\nL2422:   4. (if required) re-inserting cite1135†carried forward values in the converted colors\nL2423:   5. fill in missing components with the other color’s component values\nL2424:   6. (if required) fixing up the hues, depending on the selected cite1136†<hue-interpolation-method> L2425:   7. changing the color components to cite1137†premultiplied form\nL2426:   8. linearly interpolating each component of the computed value of the color separately\nL2427:   9. undoing cite1137†premultiplication L2503: However, user agents must handle interpolation between legacy sRGB color formats (hex colors, named colors, cite353†rgb() , cite355†hsl() or cite357†hwb() and the equivalent alpha-including forms) in gamma-encoded sRGB space. This provides Web compatibility; legacy sRGB content interpolates in the sRGB space by default.\nL2504: \nL2505: Tests\nL2506: \nL2507:   * cite1211†legacy-color-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1212†(live test)†wpt.live cite1213†(source)†github.com L2508: This also means that authors can choose to opt-in to better interpolation, even between sRGB colors, by using the non-legacy color(srgb r g b) form for at least one of their colors, or by explicitly specifying an cite1145†interpolation color space .\nL2509: \nL2510: Tests\nL2511: \nL2512:   * cite1214†css-color-4-colors-default-to-oklab-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1215†(live test)†wpt.live cite1216†(source)†github.com L2513: If the colors to be interpolated are outside the gamut of the cite1145†interpolation color space , then once converted to that space, they will contain out of range values.\nL2514: \nL2515: These are not clipped; the values must be interpolated as-is.\nL2516: ### 13.3. Interpolating with Missing Components\nL2517: \nL2518: In the course of converting the two colors to the cite1145†interpolation color space , any cite377†missing components would be replaced with the value 0.\nL2519: Thus, the first stage in interpolating two colors is to classify any cite377†missing components in the input colors, and compare them to the components of the cite1145†interpolation color space . If any cite443†analogous components which are missing components are found, they will be carried forward and re-inserted in the converted color before premultiplication, and before linear interpolation takes place.\nL2520: Similarly, if every component of an cite1134†analogous set (defined below) in the original color is a cite377†missing component , they are all cite1135†carried forward and re-inserted in the corresponding analogous set of the cite1145†interpolation color space .\nL2521: \nL2522: Alpha is its own analogous component (alpha is analogous to alpha), so a cite377†missing alpha is cite1135†carried forward in exactly the same way as any other missing component.\nL2523: \nL2524: The analogous components are as follows:\nL2525: Category  | Components\nL2526: --- | ---\nL2527: Reds  | r,x\nL2528: Greens  | g,y\nL2529: Blues  | b,z\nL2530: Lightness  | L\nL2531: Colorfulness  | C, S\nL2532: Hue  | H\nL2533: Opponent a  | a\nL2534: Opponent b  | b\nL2535: Alpha  | alpha\nL2536: \nL2537: Note: for the purposes of this classification, the XYZ spaces are considered super-saturated RGB spaces. Also, despite Saturation being Lightness-dependent, it falls in the same category as Chroma here. The Whiteness and Blackness components of HWB have no analogs in other color spaces.\nL2538: Additionally, for any two color spaces, the components that remain after removing all cite443†analogous components form an analogous set of components.\nL2539: \nL2540: Note: Because the full set of all color components is the cite1134†analogous set that remains when there are no individual cite443†analogous components , a color with all color components cite377†missing will have all color components missing in the cite1145†interpolation color space as well.\nL2541: When converting `lab(50% none none)` to LCH for interpolation, Lightness is individually cite443†analogous . The remaining components (cite1217†a and cite1218†b in Lab; cite1219†C and cite1220†H in LCH) form an cite1134†analogous set . Since both a and b are cite377†missing , both C and H are carried forward as missing, giving `lch(50% none none)` rather than `lch(50% 0 0)`.\nL2649:   * cite1150†color-mix-percents-01.html†wpt.fyi cite1151†(live test)†wpt.live cite1152†(source)†github.com L2650:   * cite1153†color-mix-percents-02.html†wpt.fyi cite1154†(live test)†wpt.live cite1155†(source)†github.com L2651: \nL2652: Note: As a reminder, if the interpolating colors were not already in the specified interpolation color space, then converting them will turn any cite383†powerless components into cite377†missing components .\nL2653: #### 13.5.1. shorter\nL2654: \nL2655: Hue angles are interpolated to take the shorter of the two arcs between the starting and ending hues.\nL2656: \nL2657: For example, the midpoint when interpolating in OkLCh from a red  oklch(0.6 0.24 30) to a yellow  oklch(0.8 0.15 90) would be at a hue angle of 30 + (90 - 30) * 0.5 = 60 degrees, along the shorter arc between the two colors, giving a deep orange  oklch(0.7 0.195 60)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHTML Standard (https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html)\nciteturn1378view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view1\",\"pattern\":\"update a style block\"}); Total lines: 1998\nL1822: The `cite399†title ` attribute on `cite22†style ` elements, like the `cite137†title ` attribute on `cite10†link ` elements, differs from the global `cite184†title ` attribute in that a `cite22†style ` block without a title does not inherit the title of the parent element: it merely has no title.\nL1823: \nL1824: The cite64†child text content†dom.spec.whatwg.org of a `cite22†style ` element must be that of a cite396†conformant style sheet†drafts.csswg.org .\nL1827: The cite412†scoping root†drafts.csswg.org of such an `cite410†@scope†drafts.csswg.org ` rule is the `cite22†style ` element's parent, so its style rules can only style the parent and the parent's descendants. Since the `cite22†style ` element has to be the first child of its parent, those descendants come after the `cite22†style ` element. This prevents a `cite22†style ` element in the `cite30†body ` from restyling content that the user agent might already have rendered, other than its parent.\nL1828: At-rules like `@font-face`, `@keyframes`, and `@property` define names for the whole document. Using such names only within the `cite22†style ` element's parent, and picking names that aren't used elsewhere, avoids restyling content that the user agent might already have rendered.\nL1829: In the following fragment, the `cite22†style ` element only styles its parent `cite413†article ` element and that element's descendants:\nL1830: \nL1831:     `<article>\nL1832:      <style>\nL1833:       @scope {\nL1834:         :scope { border: 1px solid; }\nL1835:         img { max-width: 100%; }\nL1836:       }\nL1837:      </style>\nL1838:      <h2>Kittens</h2>\nL1839:      <img src=\"kittens.jpg\" alt=\"Three kittens asleep in a basket.\">\nL1840:     </article>`\nL1841: Adding `h2 { margin: 0; }` outside the `cite410†@scope†drafts.csswg.org ` rule would make the style sheet non-conforming, even though the only `cite414†h2 ` element comes after the `cite22†style ` element.\nL1842: \nL1843: A `cite22†style ` element is cite415†implicitly potentially render-blocking if the element was created by its cite91†node document†dom.spec.whatwg.org 's parser.\nL1844: \nL1845: * * *\nL1846: \nL1847: The user agent must run the cite416†update a `style` block algorithm whenever any of the following conditions occur:\nL1848:   * The element is popped off the cite417†stack of open elements of an cite418†HTML parser or cite419†XML parser .\nL1849: \nL1850:   * The element is not on the cite417†stack of open elements of an cite418†HTML parser or cite419†XML parser , and it cite420†becomes connected or cite421†disconnected .\nL1851: \nL1852:   * The element is not on the cite417†stack of open elements of an cite418†HTML parser or cite419†XML parser , and its cite422†children changed steps†dom.spec.whatwg.org run.\nL1853: \nL1854: The update a `style` block algorithm is as follows:\nL1855:   1. Let `element` be the `cite22†style ` element.\nL1856: \nL1857:   2. If `element` has an cite407†associated CSS style sheet†drafts.csswg.org , cite423†remove the CSS style sheet†drafts.csswg.org in question.\nL1858: \nL1859:   3. If `element` is not cite424†connected†dom.spec.whatwg.org , then return.\nL1860: \nL1861:   4. If `element`'s `cite425†type ` attribute is present and its value is neither the empty string nor an cite197†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"`cite223†text/css `\", then return.\nL1862: In particular, a `cite425†type ` value with parameters, such as \"`text/css; charset=utf-8`\", will cause this algorithm to return early.\nL1863: \nL1864:   5. If the cite426†Should element's inline behavior be blocked by Content Security Policy?†w3c.github.io algorithm returns \"`Blocked`\" when executed upon `element`, \"`style`\", and `element`'s cite64†child text content†dom.spec.whatwg.org , then return. cite380†[CSP] L1865: \nL1866:   6. cite427†Create a CSS style sheet†drafts.csswg.org with the following properties:\nL1867: cite428†type†drafts.csswg.org L1868: \nL1869: \nL1870: `cite223†text/css `\nL1871: \nL1872: cite429†owner node†drafts.csswg.org L1873: \nL1874: \nL1875: `element`\nL1876: \nL1877: cite430†media†drafts.csswg.org L1878: \nL1879: \nL1880: The `cite397†media ` attribute of `element`.\nL1881: \nL1882: This is a reference to the (possibly absent at this time) attribute, rather than a copy of the attribute's current value. CSSOM defines what happens when the attribute is dynamically set, changed, or removed.\nL1883: \nL1884: cite431†title†drafts.csswg.org L1885: The `cite399†title ` attribute of `element`, if `element` is cite93†in a document tree†dom.spec.whatwg.org , or the empty string otherwise.\nL1886: \nL1887: Again, this is a reference to the attribute.\nL1888: \nL1889: cite432†alternate flag†drafts.csswg.org L1890: \nL1891: \nL1892: Unset.\nL1893: \nL1894: cite433†origin-clean flag†drafts.csswg.org L1895: \nL1896: \nL1897: Set.\nL1898: \nL1899: cite434†location†drafts.csswg.org L1900: cite435†parent CSS style sheet†drafts.csswg.org L1901: cite436†owner CSS rule†drafts.csswg.org L1902: \nL1903: \nL1904: null\nL1905: \nL1906: cite408†disabled flag†drafts.csswg.org L1907: \nL1908: \nL1909: Left at its default value.\nL1943: This specification does not specify a style system, but CSS is expected to be supported by most web browsers. cite454†[CSS] L1944: \nL1945: The `cite151†LinkStyle†drafts.csswg.org ` interface is also implemented by this element. cite362†[CSSOM] L1946: The following document has its stress emphasis styled as bright red text rather than italics text, while leaving titles of works and Latin words in their default italics. It shows how using appropriate elements enables easier restyling of documents.\nL1947: \nL1948:     `<!DOCTYPE html>\nL1949:     <html lang=\"en-US\">\nL1950:      <head>\nL1951:       <title>My favorite book</title>\nL1952:       <style>\nL1953:        body { color: black; background: white; }\nL1954:        em { font-style: normal; color: red; }\nL1955:       </style>\nL1956:      </head>\nL1957:      <body>\nL1958:       <p>My <em>favorite</em> book of all time has <em>got</em> to be\nL1959:       <cite>A Cat's Life</cite>. It is a book by P. Rahmel that talks\nL1960:       about the <i lang=\"la\">Felis catus</i> in modern human society.</p>\nL1961:      </body>\nL1962:     </html>`\nL1963: #### 4.2.7 Interactions of styling and scripting\nL1964: \nL1965: If the style sheet referenced no other resources (e.g., it was an internal style sheet given by a `cite22†style ` element with no `@import` rules), then the style rules must be cite95†immediately made available to script; otherwise, the style rules must only be made available to script once the cite455†event loop reaches its cite456†update the rendering step.\nL1966: An element `el` in the context of a `cite55†Document ` of an cite418†HTML parser or cite419†XML parser contributes a script-blocking style sheet if all of the following are true:\nL1967: \nL1968:   * `el` was created by that `cite55†Document `'s parser.\nL1969: \nL1970:   * `el` is either a `cite22†style ` element or a `cite10†link ` element that was an cite165†external resource link that contributes to the styling processing model when the `el` was created by the parser.\nL1971: \nL1972:   * `el`'s `media` attribute's value cite218†matches the environment .\nL1973:   * `el`'s style sheet was enabled when the element was created by the parser.\nL1974: \nL1975:   * The last time the cite455†event loop reached cite457†step 1 , `el`'s cite458†root†dom.spec.whatwg.org was that `cite55†Document `.\nL1976: \nL1977:   * The user agent hasn't given up on loading that particular style sheet yet. A user agent may give up on loading a style sheet at any time.\nL1978: Giving up on a style sheet before the style sheet loads, if the style sheet eventually does still load, means that the script might end up operating with incorrect information.\nL1979: For example, if a style sheet sets the color of an element to green, but a script that inspects the resulting style is executed before the sheet is loaded, the script will find that the element is black (or whatever the default color is), and might thus make poor choices (e.g., deciding to use black as the color elsewhere on the page, instead of green).\nL1980: Implementers have to balance the likelihood of a script using incorrect information with the performance impact of doing nothing while waiting for a slow network request to finish.\nL1981: It is expected that counterparts to the above rules also apply to `cite459†<?xml-stylesheet?>†www.w3.org ` PIs. However, this has not yet been thoroughly investigated.\nL1982: \nL1983: A `cite55†Document ` has a script-blocking style sheet set, which is an cite460†ordered set†infra.spec.whatwg.org , initially empty.\nL1984: \nL1985: A `cite55†Document ` `document` has a style sheet that is blocking scripts if the following steps return true:\nL1986:   1. If `document`'s cite441†script-blocking style sheet set is not cite461†empty†infra.spec.whatwg.org , then return true.\nL1987: \nL1988:   2. If `document`'s cite373†node navigable is null, then return false.\nL1989: \nL1990:   3. Let `containerDocument` be `document`'s cite373†node navigable 's cite462†container document .\nL1991: \nL1992:   4. If `containerDocument` is non-null and `containerDocument`'s cite441†script-blocking style sheet set is not cite461†empty†infra.spec.whatwg.org , then return true.\nL1993: \nL1994:   5. Return false.\nL1995: A `cite55†Document ` has no style sheet that is blocking scripts if it does not cite463†have a style sheet that is blocking scripts .\nL1996: \nL1997: cite0†← 3 Semantics, structure, and APIs of HTML documents — cite1†Table of Contents — cite2†4.3 Sections → --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHTML Standard (https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html)\nciteturn1378view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view2\",\"pattern\":\"not required\"}); Total lines: 1664\nL39:       13. cite37†15.5.13 The `marquee` element L40:       14. cite38†15.5.14 The `meter` element L41:       15. cite39†15.5.15 The `progress` element L42:       16. cite40†15.5.16 The `select` element L43:       17. cite41†15.5.17 The `textarea` element L44:     6. cite42†15.6 Frames and framesets L45:     7. cite43†15.7 Interactive media L46:       1. cite44†15.7.1 Links, forms, and navigation L47:       2. cite45†15.7.2 The `title` attribute L48:       3. cite46†15.7.3 Editing hosts L49:       4. cite47†15.7.4 Text rendered in native user interfaces L50:     8. cite48†15.8 Print media L51:     9. cite49†15.9 Unstyled XML documents L52: ## 15 Rendering\nL53: User agents are not required to present HTML documents in any particular way. However, this section provides a set of suggestions for rendering HTML documents that, if followed, are likely to lead to a user experience that closely resembles the experience intended by the documents' authors. So as to avoid confusion regarding the normativity of this section, \"must\" has not been used. Instead, the term \"expected\" is used to indicate behavior that will lead to this experience.\nL54: For the purposes of conformance for user agents designated as cite50†supporting the suggested default rendering , the term \"expected\" in this section has the same conformance implications as \"must\".\nL55: ### 15.1 Introduction\nL56: \nL57: The suggestions in this section are generally expressed in CSS terms. User agents are cite51†expected to either support CSS, or translate from the CSS rules given in this section to approximations for other presentation mechanisms.\nL58: \nL59: In the absence of style-layer rules to the contrary (e.g. author style sheets), user agents are cite51†expected to render an element so that it conveys to the user the meaning that the element cite52†represents , as described by this specification.\nL60: The suggestions in this section generally assume a visual output medium with a resolution of 96dpi or greater, but HTML is intended to apply to multiple media (it is a media-independent language). User agent implementers are encouraged to adapt the suggestions in this section to their target media.\nL61: \nL62: * * *\nL63: \nL64: An element is being rendered if it has any associated CSS layout boxes, SVG layout boxes, or some equivalent in other styling languages.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS current work & how to participate (https://www.w3.org/Style/CSS/current-work)\nciteturn1378view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view3\",\"pattern\":\"Abandoned\"}); Total lines: 247\nL165: cite417†CSS Environment Variables Level 1 | cite417†FPWD | cite418†WD†drafts.csswg.org |  | cite419†🛈 L166: c